WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:02.544
This summer, Philip and I finally managed

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:05.588
to buy a new house and
I am in love with it.

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:07.841
- But let me tell you, it wasn't easy.

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.801
We had to search for almost a year,

00:00:09.801 --> 00:00:11.386
put in multiple offers,

00:00:11.386 --> 00:00:16.099
and routinely had to go
15% above asking price.

00:00:16.099 --> 00:00:18.143
- The housing market
here in Central Texas,

00:00:18.143 --> 00:00:21.021
like most places, feels out of control.

00:00:21.021 --> 00:00:22.814
The median home price in Austin

00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:25.525
has almost doubled in the last few years,

00:00:25.525 --> 00:00:29.029
which reflects a trend in
many places across the nation.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:32.240
Some of this can be blamed
on the Coronavirus pandemic.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.993
The cost of construction
materials went way up,

00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:37.912
construction of new homes slowed way down,

00:00:37.912 --> 00:00:40.206
and a lot of people canceled their plans

00:00:40.206 --> 00:00:41.958
to put their houses on the market.

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:44.377
I mean, who would want
a bunch of strangers

00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:47.380
touring your home with a
deadly virus on the loose?

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.717
- But, like lots of
economic and social trends,

00:00:50.717 --> 00:00:54.554
COVID only accelerated things
that were already in motion.

00:00:54.554 --> 00:00:57.348
There are deeper reasons why
it's getting so expensive

00:00:57.348 --> 00:00:59.350
to buy a house in America these days

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.312
and the solutions feel pretty far off.

00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:05.023
(cheerful music)

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:13.073
- To really understand what's going on

00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:14.532
in the housing market today,

00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:16.659
we have to go back to 2008.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:19.120
When the last housing
bubble burst. (popping)

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.289
Years of reckless lending by Wall Street

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:23.666
had artificially inflated the market,

00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:26.002
sending home prices soaring.

00:01:26.002 --> 00:01:27.462
When it finally became clear

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:29.964
that too many people had
taken out predatory loans

00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:31.758
to buy overpriced houses,

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:36.012
the whole thing collapsed,
taking the U.S. economy with it.

00:01:36.012 --> 00:01:38.014
Real estate values plummeted,

00:01:38.014 --> 00:01:40.100
thousands of people lost their homes,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.060
and there were too many
houses on the market

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:44.604
with not enough people who could buy them.

00:01:44.604 --> 00:01:48.650
- When a market reacts to a
shock, it tends to overreact.

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:51.986
Construction of new homes
fell from 2 million a year

00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:54.447
to less than 500,000.

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:56.658
The decade following the great recession

00:01:56.658 --> 00:01:58.701
had the fewest number of homes built

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:01.621
than any decade since the 1960s.

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.957
- There's also fewer existing homes

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:05.416
being put up for sale.

00:02:05.416 --> 00:02:07.710
Advances in medicine and nutrition

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.839
have kept the baby boomers
healthier and more productive

00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:12.841
longer than their parents.

00:02:12.841 --> 00:02:14.509
So, they're not ready to retire,

00:02:14.509 --> 00:02:17.512
sell the house, and move to
a condo in Florida just yet.

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:18.847
- Meanwhile, millennials,

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:20.640
who've had to delay home buying

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:24.269
for financial reasons like
stagnant wages and student debt

00:02:24.269 --> 00:02:27.480
are finally ready to enter
the market in big numbers.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.482
Mortgage rates are at historic lows,

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.985
which means more people
can afford to get a loan.

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.654
The rise of remote working
means that more workers

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:37.157
want houses away from city centers,

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:40.160
but there's just not
enough units available.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.619
That means bidding wars

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:44.289
and rising prices across the country.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:47.083
- A report by the National
Association of Realtors

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:49.627
estimates that to make up the shortage,

00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:53.715
the U.S. would have to build
over 2 million homes per year

00:02:53.715 --> 00:02:55.091
for 10 years,

00:02:55.091 --> 00:02:58.303
more than we were building
during the last housing boom.

00:02:58.303 --> 00:03:00.889
So far, the rates on permits for new homes

00:03:00.889 --> 00:03:03.975
are nowhere near where they
need to be to hit that goal.

00:03:03.975 --> 00:03:05.101
- One reason is that,

00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:08.897
well, America is a little
rusty at building houses.

00:03:08.897 --> 00:03:12.150
When construction slowed
way down during the 2010s,

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.028
demand for skilled tradespeople dried up.

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:17.947
A lot of boomers retired
from careers like carpentry,

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:19.282
plumbing, and electrical,

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:20.909
and far fewer young people

00:03:20.909 --> 00:03:22.827
entered the fields to replace them.

00:03:22.827 --> 00:03:25.955
Since it can take years for
someone to become proficient,

00:03:25.955 --> 00:03:29.000
we are currently experiencing
a massive shortage

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.170
of skilled tradespeople just
when we need them the most.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.672
Developers often have
to delay construction

00:03:34.672 --> 00:03:36.007
to find professional labor,

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:39.135
sometimes even shipping
them in from other regions.

00:03:39.135 --> 00:03:42.889
This means fewer houses built
per year at higher cost.

00:03:42.889 --> 00:03:44.891
- Any cities are implementing programs

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:48.102
to attract and train new
skilled tradespeople.

00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:49.270
But in the meantime,

00:03:49.270 --> 00:03:51.022
there's a much bigger obstacle

00:03:51.022 --> 00:03:53.983
to building our way out
of this housing shortage:

00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:55.693
zoning laws.

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:56.611
Believe it or not,

00:03:56.611 --> 00:03:58.279
in most of the United States,

00:03:58.279 --> 00:04:00.406
it's illegal to build anything

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:03.618
but single-family detached homes.

00:04:03.618 --> 00:04:06.663
No duplexes, no condos, no apartments,

00:04:06.663 --> 00:04:08.456
not even a granny flat.

00:04:08.456 --> 00:04:11.459
- Many of these laws date
back to the early 20th century

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:14.462
and allowed white homeowners
to stop any development

00:04:14.462 --> 00:04:17.548
they feared would lower the
value of their property.

00:04:17.548 --> 00:04:19.676
Today, these laws are strictest

00:04:19.676 --> 00:04:22.470
in big wealthy cities, like San Francisco,

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.389
New York, and Seattle.

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:26.975
- Here in Austin, there
has been a long campaign

00:04:26.975 --> 00:04:29.352
to try to rezone much of the city

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:33.564
to allow for denser residential
areas called CodeNEXT,

00:04:33.564 --> 00:04:36.818
but it's been stuck in
legal limbo for years

00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:38.778
due to the tireless opposition

00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:41.781
of mostly white affluent homeowners

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:43.658
who want to preserve the look

00:04:43.658 --> 00:04:46.160
and demographics of their neighborhoods,

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.496
regardless of skyrocketing home prices

00:04:48.496 --> 00:04:51.291
and surging houseless population.

00:04:51.291 --> 00:04:54.419
This social group also tends
to have the highest turnout

00:04:54.419 --> 00:04:57.255
in local elections across the country.

00:04:57.255 --> 00:05:00.633
So, if you wanna serve another
term on the city council,

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:03.803
you better vote against that
affordable apartment building.

00:05:03.803 --> 00:05:05.888
- Law professor David Schleicher argues

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:07.432
that these zoning restrictions

00:05:07.432 --> 00:05:11.436
impact the overall economy
as well by suppressing wages.

00:05:11.436 --> 00:05:13.855
For example, when an area experiences

00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:15.857
a period of economic growth,

00:05:15.857 --> 00:05:18.818
the higher wages should
attract job seekers

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:21.571
from across the socioeconomic spectrum.

00:05:21.571 --> 00:05:24.782
But if zoning laws prohibit
the construction of new homes,

00:05:24.782 --> 00:05:27.285
the only people who can
afford to relocate there

00:05:27.285 --> 00:05:28.953
are already wealthy.

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:32.248
In other words, if you're a
janitor living in Reno, Nevada,

00:05:32.248 --> 00:05:34.709
you could conceivably
make more as a janitor

00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:37.879
in Silicon Valley, except
there's no housing there

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:39.172
in your price range.

00:05:39.172 --> 00:05:41.507
- California is a prime example

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.010
of the dangers of NIMBYism,

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.471
short for "Not in my backyard!"

00:05:46.471 --> 00:05:49.098
Some even call it a BANANA state:

00:05:49.098 --> 00:05:53.478
build absolutely nothing
anywhere near anyone.

00:05:53.478 --> 00:05:56.856
Despite a decade of high
output and employment,

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.566
skyrocketing home prices

00:05:58.566 --> 00:06:01.736
have forced many middle-
and low-wage workers

00:06:01.736 --> 00:06:03.780
to relocate to other states.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:06.991
What should have been a
boom economy for everyone

00:06:06.991 --> 00:06:10.745
was only available to the
affluent educated professionals

00:06:10.745 --> 00:06:12.789
who could afford to enter bidding wars

00:06:12.789 --> 00:06:16.125
over the small inventory
of existing homes.

00:06:16.125 --> 00:06:18.544
- NIMBYism also affects our ability

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.713
to build infrastructure in America.

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:23.216
One mile of commuter rail in the U.S.

00:06:23.216 --> 00:06:26.552
can be several times more
expensive than in Europe.

00:06:26.552 --> 00:06:28.763
One reason is that European governments

00:06:28.763 --> 00:06:30.390
typically have more power

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:33.184
to plan and execute
infrastructure projects

00:06:33.184 --> 00:06:34.310
from start to finish.

00:06:34.310 --> 00:06:37.438
In America, even us small
group of local homeowners

00:06:37.438 --> 00:06:40.024
can derail any building
proposal they don't like,

00:06:40.024 --> 00:06:41.401
often using historical

00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:44.404
or environmental concerns
as justifications.

00:06:44.404 --> 00:06:46.364
These delays and legal battles

00:06:46.364 --> 00:06:49.617
drastically drive up the
cost of construction.

00:06:49.617 --> 00:06:51.828
- Organizations from the Urban Institute

00:06:51.828 --> 00:06:53.830
to the National Association of Realtors

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.123
are calling on governments

00:06:55.123 --> 00:06:58.126
to make it easier to
build new construction.

00:06:58.126 --> 00:07:00.378
President Biden has proposed including

00:07:00.378 --> 00:07:03.131
in his infrastructure
bill, financial incentives

00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:06.551
to areas that are willing
to relax their zoning laws.

00:07:06.551 --> 00:07:08.177
Though, that may not be enough

00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:10.263
to persuade wealthier school districts

00:07:10.263 --> 00:07:11.889
that don't need the money.

00:07:11.889 --> 00:07:14.434
- One possible solution
to the housing shortage

00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:17.520
is for the fed to raise interest rates.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:19.564
If mortgages are more expensive,

00:07:19.564 --> 00:07:21.816
fewer people will want to buy a house.

00:07:21.816 --> 00:07:24.861
But the lack of housing affects
the rental market as well.

00:07:24.861 --> 00:07:27.947
And simply making it harder
for people to become homeowners

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:30.616
doesn't address the underlying problem.

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:32.994
- Owning a home is
still the primary method

00:07:32.994 --> 00:07:35.830
of building wealth for the
vast majority of Americans.

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:37.957
It's something to pass on to your children

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:40.376
and offers the security
and freedom of knowing

00:07:40.376 --> 00:07:42.670
that you're not at the
mercy of a landlord.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.839
- In some ways, the problem is simple.

00:07:44.839 --> 00:07:47.133
There aren't enough homes to go around,

00:07:47.133 --> 00:07:50.761
but fixing it may require
a fundamental power shift

00:07:50.761 --> 00:07:55.016
in who gets to decide what
gets built and what doesn't.

00:07:55.016 --> 00:07:57.059
- [Julia And Philip] And
that's our two cents.

00:07:57.101 --> 00:07:58.519
Thanks to our patrons

00:07:58.519 --> 00:08:00.980
for keeping Two Cents financially healthy.

